🚽 Test case: round-21 review finding #1 -- VAR_VOID now means both "no
🚽 value" (base type void, pointer_level 0) and the base type of `void *`
🚽 (VAR_VOID, pointer_level > 0), and require_value_expression() checked
🚽 only the base type, rejecting a `skibidi *p` (void*) variable as if it
🚽 held no value at all. test_ptr_source() (tests/stdrot/testptr.c)
🚽 really returns STDROT_PTR; `skibidi *p = test_ptr_source();` is a
🚽 coherent declaration under the (base type, pointer_level) model
🚽 check_type_compatibility_ex() already uses. Using `p` as a binary-
🚽 operator operand (`p != 0`) exercises require_value_expression()
🚽 directly -- confirmed via a temporary A/B test (dropping the pointer_
🚽 level check) that this was previously rejected as "Native call result
🚽 (void) cannot be used in a binary operator operand context."
skibidi main {
    skibidi *p = test_ptr_source();
    cap has_value = p != 0;
    yappin("%d\n", has_value);
    bussin 0;
}
